WEBVTT - the world, read out as sound, for those who would sooner read it

NOTE
Captions for the recording, which lasts thirteen seconds and has no words
in it until the last two. They were prepared by somebody who listened to
it a great many times and is not expected to recover the taste for music.

NOTE
The convention is the usual one: sounds go in square brackets, speech
goes without. There are eight cues. Seven of them are in square brackets.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
[the western edge. a murmur, as of sea being read aloud]

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.600
[the Flats. the same murmur, later in the afternoon]

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.600
[rounded hills, in rows. each one sounds like the one before]

NOTE
The next cue is the loud one. Listeners who had turned it up to hear the
Flats are asked to accept the captioner's apology, which was written in
advance and is all that could be done.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.400 line:10%
[THE HASH GOES BY]

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.000
[the Colons. something is about to be explained]

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
[it is not explained]

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
[the night side, hardly said at all]

NOTE
Three seconds is a long time to leave one caption on the glass. Nothing
was found to replace it with. The Deep was listened to at full volume for
a ship's lamp and the captioner reports only their own pulse.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000 align:center
<i>hello world</i>

NOTE
That was the greeting. It is the only cue here that is speech, and it is
set in italics because that is what is done for a voice from off screen.
There is no screen. The voice is from off it regardless.

NOTE
No cue is given for the silence afterwards, which is not part of the
recording and goes on for considerably longer.
